// ⚠⚠ THIS FILE WAS REWRITTEN, AS ITS OWN PREVIOUS HEADER REQUIRED. It used to
// assert an MLIR VERIFIER message — `'arith.cmpi' op operand #0 must be
// signless-integer-like, but got '!llvm.ptr'` — because no layer between the
// `where` clause and `arith.cmpi` asked whether a tuple column has an order.
// The whole cost was the diagnostic: nothing unsound shipped, no object was
// written, and the compiler reported a type-system invariant violation about
// ITSELF, dumping ~21 000 lines into `logos-mlir-verify-fail.mlir` beside it.
// That header said, verbatim, that when this was fixed the file would go red and
// MUST BE REWRITTEN, NOT DELETED. It was fixed by the column-DECLARATION refusal
// (`reflect::column_decl_admit`); this is the rewrite, and the MLIR side effect
// is gone with it.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS CELL PINS THAT ITS SIBLINGS DO NOT: THE LAYER. A tuple column is
// refused from every other clause position too, and before this change those
// positions died one layer earlier, inside a generated blob
// (`<metaprog-blob-subst>: … method 'Vec__push' arg 1: expected i64, got (i64,
// &[u8])`) — that route is fail/wql_column_decl_tuple_col_fail. `where … <` was
// the ONE position that travelled all the way to MLIR, and this file is the
// witness that it no longer does. A refusal that moves back out of the front end
// into a blob or into the verifier is a REGRESSION even though all three are
// red, and only a fixture that remembers the old layer can tell those apart.
//
// THE MECHANISM, unchanged — what the refusal removed is its REACH, not its
// existence. `el::el_ty_of_name` launders every name the lattice does not admit
// to `EL_TY_INT`, so `(i64, str)` was spelled INT by the time the relational
// operator was emitted, and INT's own `<` is an integer compare, here over a
// POINTER. `EL_ORD_NO_CMP` exists as a lattice row for `String`, but `String` is
// one named member of an UNBOUNDED class — every type whose `<` mlir-gen does
// not lower — and the laundering happened one step EARLIER than the row was
// consulted. The refusal now sits one step earlier still, at the column's
// DECLARATION, where the type NODE is in hand and the name is not the question.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct S { pub x: (i64, str), pub id: i64 }

pub deem q(ss: &[S]) { from ss s where s.x < (2i64, "b") select s.id }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
